1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,050
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:05,770
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:12,010
My name is Catherine and today we're talking a little bit about going to sleep or not being able to sleep.

4
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:12,830
That's right.

5
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:22,110
We are having some sleeping problems and we're actually going to check out a couple of words related to this topic right now on Vocabulary Preview.

6
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:25,440
Vocabulary Preview.

7
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:32,680
Okay, so the first word is actually the technical word that describes being unable to sleep.

8
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:33,490
It's called insomnia.

9
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:34,470
Right.

10
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:38,850
So if you have insomnia, it means that you can't fall asleep.

11
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:39,650
You can't sleep.

12
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:40,370
Okay.

13
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:41,990
So this is a pretty serious condition.

14
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:48,550
For example, if you have insomnia and you don't sleep for three or four nights, you're not going to do very well at work, right?

15
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:49,170
Right.

16
00:00:49,170 --> 00:00:52,490
Or if you're studying, you're tired at school, you can't concentrate.

17
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:53,990
So it's a very big problem.

18
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:55,750
What's that other word though?

19
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,790
We have insomnia, but we also have another interesting disorder.

20
00:00:59,400 --> 00:00:59,570
Right.

21
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:07,880
If somebody is able to sleep but walks while they're sleeping, that person is called a sleepwalker.

22
00:01:08,010 --> 00:01:08,650
That's right.

23
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,250
And you don't actually just have to walk to be a sleepwalker.

24
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:17,570
You can do any number of strange things like basically acting like you're awake when you're not.

25
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:19,280
Exactly.

26
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,760
So some people get up and walk, actually go outside.

27
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:24,350
Some people get dressed.

28
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:26,480
Some people go downstairs and cook.

29
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:27,190
Yeah, that's right.

30
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,200
And the worst is the people who eat because they gain weight and they don't know why.

31
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:31,390
Exactly.

32
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,520
So sleepwalking is what you do.

33
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,350
And if you do this, you're called a sleepwalker.

34
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:38,050
Okay.

35
00:01:38,170 --> 00:01:39,630
Sleepwalk is the verb.

36
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:42,150
Sleepwalker is the noun or the person.

37
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:42,910
All right.

38
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:47,650
So we've taken a look at two words that we're going to find in our dialogue today.

39
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:50,970
Why don't we go now and listen to the dialogue for the first time.

40
00:01:57,710 --> 00:01:59,490
Alex, what's up with you?

41
00:02:00,070 --> 00:02:00,830
You look dreadful.

42
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:02,030
Hey, Jill.

43
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:03,490
I don't know.

44
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:06,510
I've been having trouble sleeping these past few weeks.

45
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:09,750
I usually lie in bed for hours trying to get to sleep.

46
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:13,730
I've tried stretching and different breathing techniques before going to bed.

47
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:16,510
I've tried eating and not eating different foods.

48
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:18,890
I've even tried counting sheep.

49
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:23,660
And then when I finally get to sleep, I have these really disturbing nightmares.

50
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:27,660
So I usually wake up in a panic and more tired than before I went to sleep.

51
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:29,520
Wow.

52
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:30,720
Maybe you should get that checked out.

53
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:32,020
Maybe you're stressed.

54
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,080
Just take some sedatives.

55
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:34,960
Works for me.

56
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:39,680
Every so often having some melatonin on hand helps me when I have trouble sleeping.

57
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:42,000
It works on all kinds of sleeping disorders.

58
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,560
It's the stuff pilots use to regulate their sleeping patterns.

59
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:46,940
I heard of that.

60
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,300
But does that apply to Alex's situation?

61
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:50,720
Yeah, sure.

62
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:52,320
Why not?

63
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:57,990
Sounds like he only has transient insomnia since it's a recent thing so taking melatonin should do the trick.

64
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:01,410
But shouldn't he be looking into why it's been happening?

65
00:03:01,790 --> 00:03:03,470
Well, aren't you the little psychologist?

66
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:06,010
Our buddy's having trouble sleeping.

67
00:03:06,450 --> 00:03:07,410
It's easy and curable.

68
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:10,490
It's not something freakish like if he was a sleepwalker.

69
00:03:10,910 --> 00:03:12,710
Well, there's that too.

70
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:19,360
Alright we're back.

71
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:26,100
So obviously this person not only has insomnia but also has nightmares, sleepwalks, a mess.

72
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:27,200
That's right.

73
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,780
And you can tell when someone hasn't been sleeping well.

74
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:36,820
They have that look like there's bags under their eyes or they just look really tired.

75
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,800
And so the friend here says, Alex, you look dreadful.

76
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:42,640
That's right, dreadful.

77
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:47,310
So this is an adjective and it's a way to describe someone that looks terrible.

78
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:48,470
You look horrible.

79
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:49,550
You look tired.

80
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:50,410
You look sleepy.

81
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:51,360
That's right.

82
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:52,680
So this is not a compliment.

83
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:55,620
This is something that you say when you're worried about someone.

84
00:03:56,220 --> 00:04:00,320
So for example, if someone's really, really tired, you say, you look dreadful.

85
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:07,180
Or if someone is wearing very dirty clothes, they don't look like they went home yesterday, you say, what's wrong?

86
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:07,880
You look dreadful.

87
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:09,400
Right.

88
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:12,920
I think you can also use it to describe objects or maybe food, right?

89
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:14,880
This lasagna is dreadful.

90
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:16,280
It means it's very bad.

91
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,720
And you don't want to say that to the person who cooked it, of course.

92
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:19,220
Exactly.

93
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:26,520
Alright, so the person obviously because is suffering from insomnia looks dreadful.

94
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:30,400
And he was explaining what he does to try to fall asleep.

95
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:37,090
For example, counting sheep, stretching, and he also said that he tried different breathing techniques.

96
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:42,300
Okay, so basically a technique is an approach to something, a kind of method.

97
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:45,240
But in this case, we're talking about breathing techniques.

98
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:52,560
So for some people, one breathing technique is breathing in through your nose and breathing out through your mouth.

99
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:54,310
Like that.

100
00:04:54,530 --> 00:04:55,650
That's one kind of technique.

101
00:04:56,190 --> 00:04:58,910
So a technique, as you say, is a way of doing something.

102
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:03,600
So breathing techniques are different ways of breathing.

103
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:07,230
So maybe you will relax, so maybe you'll fall asleep, things like this.

104
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:07,690
Exactly.

105
00:05:08,010 --> 00:05:10,630
It's one of those things that you might learn about in a yoga class, for example.

106
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,640
There's different breathing techniques.

107
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:12,850
Right.

108
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:18,040
And you can combine techniques with other words as well, adding the ing.

109
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:23,440
For example, you have walking techniques, maybe to put less stress on your knees.

110
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:26,440
Or running techniques, swimming techniques.

111
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:29,160
I think there's a technique for almost everything.

112
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:32,040
Just about anything you can do, there's a technique for it.

113
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,520
Right.

114
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:34,520
Yeah, yeah, for sure.

115
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:35,520
All right.

116
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:41,780
And well, moving on, he was talking about his nightmares and he described his nightmares as disturbing.

117
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,560
He has disturbing nightmares.

118
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:50,400
Okay, well, I think this is an important word to cover here, Marco, because we've heard the verb to disturb before.

119
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:52,960
Like I'm sorry to disturb you.

120
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:54,840
It means I'm sorry to bother you.

121
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:56,680
But disturbing is an adjective.

122
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:59,680
It describes something that makes me very uncomfortable.

123
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:00,680
Right?

124
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:01,680
Right.

125
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:09,640
So, for example, a lot of times in the news, we see very disturbing images, car accidents or fires or bombs.

126
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:12,880
And what's disturbing is when you see human beings who have been hurt.

127
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:17,510
So images that make you really uncomfortable or sad, these are disturbing.

128
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:18,410
Exactly.

129
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:24,240
So disturbing nightmares are nightmares that are not very pleasant.

130
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,320
They make him scared, maybe, or uncomfortable.

131
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:27,320
Right.

132
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:29,240
Like a dream, for example, where your parents die.

133
00:06:29,470 --> 00:06:30,150
It's pretty disturbing.

134
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:30,690
Exactly.

135
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
All right.

136
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,350
And well, one of his friends recommended, you know what?

137
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:36,030
Stop with all of this.

138
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:37,590
Just take some sedatives.

139
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:38,880
Okay.

140
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:45,580
So sedatives are a kind of medicine that help a person relax or that help make a person tired.

141
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:46,240
Right.

142
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:49,340
So I think it comes from the verb to sedate.

143
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:49,940
Yes.

144
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:55,880
And basically that is to calm down, to tranquilize.

145
00:06:55,880 --> 00:06:56,880
That's right.

146
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:00,460
So in this case, we're talking about a person, but you can also give an animal sedatives.

147
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:05,260
For example, if you want to take your dog on an airplane, the dog needs to relax.

148
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:06,540
You give it some sedatives.

149
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:07,180
Exactly.

150
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:13,140
And I think when he mentioned a specific name of a sedative, it's melatonin.

151
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:14,040
That's right.

152
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:21,320
So melatonin, melatonin is a chemical that our bodies naturally produce that help calm us down.

153
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:28,240
And so a lot of pharmaceutical companies or drug companies sell melatonin in a pill form.

154
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,240
It's basically, it's a chemical that helps you relax.

155
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:31,400
Exactly.

156
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:37,920
So obviously if you take melatonin, you're going to feel sleepy or you're going to be very relaxed and you're going to fall asleep a lot easier.

157
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:39,000
Well, hopefully.

158
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:40,200
Hopefully.

159
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,200
That's right.

160
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:47,840
So, and well, the whole thing that we've been talking about, insomnia, sleepwalking, these are called sleeping disorders.

161
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:48,680
Right.

162
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:51,160
And remember, you might also hear the term sleep disorders.

163
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:52,980
They mean problems with sleeping.

164
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:59,240
So people who either cannot fall asleep or people who have bad dreams or people who sleep walk.

165
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:00,040
Exactly.

166
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:05,520
So a disorder is something of an illness or a condition that is not normal.

167
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:12,160
So for example, as you said, a sleeping disorder or a sleep disorder, it means that you're not able to sleep properly or normally.

168
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:14,460
But there are other kinds of disorders, right?

169
00:08:14,810 --> 00:08:15,130
Right.

170
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:18,840
And many people have heard of eating disorders.

171
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:19,840
Okay.

172
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:21,670
So that's basically people who do not eat like everyone else.

173
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Exactly.

174
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,530
So that's what happens with a disorder.

175
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:25,560
All right.

176
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:27,090
So we've taken a look at a lot of vocab.

177
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,570
Now it's time for us to take a look at Fluency Builder.

178
00:08:29,750 --> 00:08:32,290
But before that, why don't we listen to the dialogue again?

179
